add('gautam-adani-1',
 'Gautam Adani is the subject of three separate legal tracks that must not be merged. (1) The alleged 2020–2024 Indian power-contract bribery scheme: the SEC alleged that he and Sagar Adani orchestrated promises of hundreds of millions of dollars in bribes and misled US bond investors; in May 2026 both consented to SEC judgments without admitting or denying (USD 6 million and USD 12 million penalties). In the criminal case, a federal judge in August 2026 dismissed with prejudice the three counts charged against Gautam Adani (securities-fraud conspiracy, wire-fraud conspiracy, securities fraud) on the Justice Department’s motion, expressly taking no view of the merits and calling the process irregular; the FCPA and obstruction counts, charged only against other, non-appearing defendants, remained undecided at the last reviewed order. No court has found that bribes were paid. (2) The Hindenburg allegations: SEBI’s September 2025 final order found them not established; its September 2026 order found minimum-public-shareholding and fraud allegations not established against twelve noticees (including Vinod Adani) while the group companies and Gautam Adani settled separately without admission. (3) A separate USD 275 million OFAC sanctions settlement by Adani Enterprises, unrelated to the bribery allegation.',
 'Alleged payments to Indian state officials → above-market power-purchase commitments (unadjudicated); separately, documented 2026 US process questions about dismissal',
 'Alleged conduct 2020–2024; US charges and SEC complaint 20 Nov 2024; SEC consent 14 May 2026; DOJ dismissal motion 18 May 2026; court order Aug 2026; SEBI orders Sept 2025 and Sept 2026',
 'Moderate / legal status well documented; underlying allegation never adjudicated',
 [r('The SEC alleged that Gautam Adani and Sagar Adani orchestrated a scheme to pay or promise hundreds of millions of dollars in bribes to Indian officials in exchange for commitments to buy energy at above-market rates benefiting Adani Green, and falsely touted anti-bribery compliance in a September 2021 bond offering. Each consented to judgment, subject to court approval, without admitting or denying; penalties USD 6 million (Gautam) and USD 12 million (Sagar). Case 1:24-cv-08080 (EDNY).','SEC Litigation Release 26554',SECLR,'2026-05-14','Regulator allegation and no-admission consent settlement','Full release text read'),
  r('Garaufis’s order summarizes the October 2024 indictment: it alleged about USD 265 million in bribes for solar contracts, a financing-fraud scheme and an obstruction scheme. Count One (FCPA conspiracy) was charged only against non-appearing defendants; Gautam Adani, Sagar Adani and Vneet Jaain were charged in Counts Two to Four (securities-fraud conspiracy, wire-fraud conspiracy, securities fraud). These are charging-document allegations recited by the court, not findings. The order also records that Adani Green is India-based and that the SEC complaints allege bribes to Indian state officials.','US v. Adani, 24-CR-433 (NGG), Memorandum & Order (Dkt. 49)',ORDER,'2026-08-10','Court recital of charges (allegation, not finding)','Full 47-page text extracted and read; the order’s printed date is illegible in the text layer, 10 August 2026 is per the SEC notice and press reports'),
  r('The same order records that counsel for Adani presented about 600 pages to DOJ, that Principal Associate Deputy AG R. Trent McCotter says he was the sole decision-maker and worked with defense counsel, and that defense counsel twice told DOJ a USD 10 billion US investment could be part of a resolution. The EDNY US Attorney wrote that this proposal was categorically rejected by his office; McCotter says it played no role; Adani swore he knew of no agreement. The judge called the irregularities concerning but stated that the ruling expresses no opinion on the merits.','US v. Adani, Memorandum & Order, sections I.D–I.F and Conclusion',ORDER,'2026-08-10','Court record of process (documented, not adjudicated as an exchange)','Full text read'),
  r('DOJ’s one-paragraph Rule 48(a) motion asked to dismiss the whole indictment with prejudice, citing prosecutorial discretion “not to devote further resources” to charges against individuals; counsel for all defendants consented; signed by McCotter and US Attorney Joseph Nocella Jr.','DOJ Rule 48(a) motion (Dkt. 23)',MOT,'2026-05-18','Primary court filing','Full 2-page filing and proposed order text read'),
  r('On 8 July 2026 the court noted McCotter’s letter raised, for the first time, the possibility of an unmemorialized agreement tied to the dismissal and ordered Gautam Adani to answer by sworn affidavit whether anything was promised, offered or agreed in connection with the dismissal.','US v. Adani, Order (Dkt. 38)',JUL8,'2026-07-08','Primary court order','Full 4-page text read'),
  r('OFAC announced a USD 275 million settlement with Adani Enterprises Limited for 32 apparent Iran-sanctions violations (LPG purchases, about USD 192 million in payments, November 2023–June 2025), found egregious and not voluntarily self-disclosed. This is a corporate sanctions matter, not evidence for the bribery or political-exchange mechanism; it is excluded from the rubric.','US Treasury OFAC recent actions, 18 May 2026',OFAC,'2026-05-18','Separate corporate enforcement (excluded from rubric)','Notice page read; the linked enforcement release PDF was not opened')],
 [r('Reuters reports Judge Garaufis dismissed the criminal case on 10 August 2026 at prosecutors’ request, said he was satisfied the USD 10 billion pledge did not factor into the decision, and criticized the process. Adani: “I welcome the U.S. Court’s decision with humility and deep respect for the judicial process.” The Adani Group denies wrongdoing.','Reuters via Investing.com',REUT,'2026-08-10','Subject response and material case update','Syndicated summary read; full Reuters text not available'),
  r('The court granted the motion only in part: Counts Two to Four against Gautam Adani, Sagar Adani and Jaain were dismissed with prejudice; judgment was reserved on Counts One and Five against five non-appearing defendants pending DOJ reasons by 31 August 2026. Footnote 44: no one should take the ruling as agreement with DOJ’s decision or an opinion on the merits. No later filing was found in this review.','US v. Adani, Memorandum & Order: disposition',ORDER,'2026-08-10','Legal status (not a finding of innocence or guilt)','Full text read'),
  r('The SEC’s covered-action notice lists SEC v. Gautam Adani and Sagar Adani (24-cv-08080) with a qualifying judgment/order date of 10 August 2026; the May release stated the consents were subject to court approval. The notice does not state the type of judgment, so entry of the consent judgments is inferred, not confirmed.','SEC covered-action notice 2026-071',SECN,'2026-09-30','Civil status update','Notice page read'),
  r('The Adani Group called the DOJ and SEC allegations against Adani Green directors baseless and denied, noted the charges are unproven and defendants presumed innocent, and said it would pursue all legal remedies.','b360 Nepal report of the Adani Group statement',B360,'2024-11-22','Subject response','Short secondary report read; the original statement was not located'),
  r('SEBI’s final order (Kamlesh C. Varshney, whole-time member) found the Hindenburg-linked allegations about transactions with Milestone Tradelinks and Rehvar Infrastructure not established against Adani Ports, Adani Power, Adani Enterprises, Gautam Adani and others: the entities were not related parties under rules then in force, there was no allegation of siphoning, and the money had come back with interest before the investigation. Proceedings disposed of without direction.','SEBI final order WTM/KV/CFID/CFID-SEC1/31672/2025-26',SEBI25,'2025-09-18','Regulatory finding (corporate and personal noticees)','Full 44-page order read'),
  r('The Supreme Court refused to transfer the Hindenburg-related investigation to an SIT or the CBI, found no regulatory failure attributable to SEBI, said third-party reports could not be conclusive proof of inadequate investigation, and directed SEBI and agencies to probe whether Hindenburg’s and others’ short positions involved any infraction. It did not adjudicate the Adani bribery allegations, which arose later.','Vishal Tiwari v. Union of India, 2024 INSC 3 (SEBI-hosted copy)',SC24,'2024-01-03','Court judgment (separate Hindenburg matter)','Full 46-page text read'),
  r('SEBI’s 28 September 2026 final order found the minimum-public-shareholding and PFUTP fraud allegations not established against Noticees 1–12 (including Vinod Adani and offshore investors) and penalised two of them for failing to furnish information. Noticees 13–30, including the four listed companies and Gautam Adani, had settled under SEBI’s settlement regulations by separate order, so this finding does not decide their case.','SEBI final order WTM/KV/IVD-3/ID2/32734/2026-27',SEBI26,'2026-09-28','Regulatory finding and settlement context','Full 81-page order read; the separate settlement order was not opened'),
  r('PTI reports Gautam Adani, four group companies and others settled the MPS case for Rs 1.48 crore without admitting or denying; a week earlier five group companies settled a related-party-disclosure adjudication for about Rs 1.5 crore. Settlements are not findings of guilt or innocence.','PTI via The New Indian Express',NIE,'2026-09-28','Settlement report (secondary)','Article text read; the underlying settlement orders were not opened'),
  r('Hindenburg’s January 2023 report alleged stock manipulation, offshore shell networks and accounting issues, and disclosed a short position. It alleged market conduct, not bribery of officials; it is context for the SEBI outcomes above, not support for the political-exchange mechanism.','Hindenburg Research report',HIND,'2023-01-24','Short-seller allegation (context)','First 100,000 characters of the page read; the later part was not read')],
 'Scope: only the alleged Indian power-contract bribery mechanism and the 2026 US dismissal process are scored. Gautam Adani personally was not charged in the FCPA count; the SEC complaint does attribute orchestration to him. The criminal case is not fully closed on the record read (Counts One and Five, other defendants) and no filing after 12 August 2026 was found. A consent judgment’s entry is inferred, not confirmed. No Indian criminal proceeding, CAG report, Parliament answer or the alleged political relationships with Indian governments were reviewed; Sagar Adani, Vinod Adani and Adani companies are separate persons. Neither the dismissal nor the settlements nor the SEBI findings determine the wider political-economy question, and the allegation is not a finding of bribery.',
 rubric=([2,2,2],[15,80],
  ['The SEC and indictment allege payments to Indian state officials; the allegation is specific but contested and unadjudicated.','Above-market energy-purchase commitments for Adani Green are specifically alleged; no court found them.','The alleged exchange is specific, but no finding, an unadmitted civil settlement and a criminal dismissal on which the judge took no merits view limit inference; the 2026 investment-pledge discussions are documented but disputed and unadjudicated.'],
  [[0,1],[0,1],[0,1,2,3,4]]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- MITTAL
H13='https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/2002/feb/13/engagements'
H5='https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/2002/mar/05/steel-industry'
KRY='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kryvorizhstal'
RFE='https://www.rferl.org/amp/kazakhstan-arcelormittal-mining-tragedy/32660454.html'
