IW='https://www.industryweek.com/the-economy/emerging-markets/article/21960748/arcelormittal-scraps-india-steel-plant-plan'
add('lakshmi-mittal',
 'Forbes lists Lakshmi Mittal under India, but his business is global, so each episode has its own jurisdiction. The scored episode is UK/Romania, 2001–2002: Tony Blair signed a letter supporting Mittal’s company LNM in Romania’s Sidex privatization while Mittal was a £125,000 Labour donor. The overlap is documented; the government said the donation was unrelated and the award decision preceded the letter, and the Commons rejected the opposition motion. Later episodes point the other way and are not averaged into the score: a live-broadcast 2005 Ukrainian re-auction (Ukraine), an Odisha steel plant abandoned in 2013 over delayed iron-ore blocks (India), and the 2023 Kazakhstan transfer of ArcelorMittal Temirtau to the state after a mine disaster (Kazakhstan). No UK/EU case or Indian coal-block case involving Mittal was found in this review.',
 'Party donation and diplomatic endorsement ↔ disputed influence on a privatization (UK/Romania 2001–02)',
 'Sidex privatization and Blair letter, July–November 2001; Commons scrutiny February–March 2002; contrast episodes 2005 (Ukraine), 2013 (India), 2023 (Kazakhstan)',
 'Moderate / historical single-episode score',
 [r('Blair told the Commons it was public record that Mittal was a Labour donor, that the letter he signed mentioned LNM rather than Mittal, and that knowing of the support would have made no difference. The Conservative leader’s question confirms the letter to the Romanian prime minister was signed on 23 July 2001.','Hansard, 13 February 2002 (Prime Minister’s Questions): Blair and Duncan Smith',H13,'2002-02-13','Parliamentary record: acknowledgment of letter and donor status','Page text of the Commons sitting read on the historic-Hansard mirror'),
  r('The opposition motion (moved by Adam Price) noted the £125,000 Labour donation and Mittal’s non-domiciled status and demanded an inquiry; in debate it was argued that the letter followed within days of the request and that Romania sold Sidex in November after an EBRD loan. These are political arguments, not findings.','Hansard, 5 March 2002: Steel Industry (Opposition Day)',H5,'2002-03-05','Parliamentary debate: allegation by opposition','Full debate text read on the historic-Hansard mirror')],
 [r('Blair said the letter was written at the British embassy’s request to support Romanian reform and, citing the Romanian prime minister, that the award decision was made before the letter; he refused an inquiry.','Hansard, 13 February 2002: Blair’s response',H13,'2002-02-13','Subject response and alternative explanation','Page text read'),
  r('Wales Secretary Paul Murphy said the donation had nothing to do with the letter, that the letter was sent after the Sidex decision but before signing, and that the EBRD loan reflected its development mandate. Both the motion (192 to 318) and, on the government amendment, the vote (306 to 181) went the government’s way. A Commons vote is not an adjudication.','Hansard, 5 March 2002: Murphy and divisions',H5,'2002-03-05','Government response and political outcome','Full debate text and division counts read'),
  r('In a live-broadcast 2005 auction in Ukraine, Mittal Steel won Kryvorizhstal for about USD 4.8 billion after a court annulled an earlier 2004 sale that had been criticized as corrupt; higher bids in 2004 had been rejected. This is the opposite pattern to patronage, in a different jurisdiction.','Wikipedia: Kryvorizhstal (secondary compilation of Kyiv Post and others)',KRY,'2005-10-24','Contrasting episode (secondary)','Page text read; underlying sources not opened'),
  r('In October 2023 Kazakhstan’s president ordered cooperation with ArcelorMittal to cease after a mine explosion killed 46, and ArcelorMittal signed an agreement to transfer ArcelorMittal Temirtau to the state; RFE/RL says Nazarbayev had enticed Mittal to revive the steelworks, but describes increasingly hostile later official treatment. The company said it would compensate victims and cooperate with the state. This shows state power over the company in Kazakhstan, not a finding on earlier relations.','RFE/RL and Business Recorder',RFE,'2023-10-30','Contrasting episode (Kazakhstan)','Both articles read; Business Recorder at '+BREC),
  r('ArcelorMittal dropped a planned 12-million-tonne steel plant in Odisha, India, saying delays in land acquisition and allocation of captive iron-ore blocks made it unviable. That is evidence against preferential Indian state benefit in that project, not a general finding.','AFP via IndustryWeek',IW,'2013-07-18','Contrasting episode (India)','Article text read')],
 'Historical single-episode estimate for UK/Romania 2001–02, not a global or present-day assessment, and not a finding that any contract or letter was purchased. Hansard records assertions, not adjudication; the Romanian procurement file and later ministerial inquiries were not reviewed; the earlier receipts from UK Parliament’s own site were replaced by the identical Commons sittings on the historic-Hansard mirror because the original host blocked automated access. The contrast episodes involve other jurisdictions and are secondary or brief. Rampia/Dipside coal-block, EU merger or antitrust matters and Mittal’s UK donations after 2002 were not reviewed.',
 rubric=([3,2,2],[10,75],
  ['The Prime Minister’s signed support letter and Mittal’s donor status are acknowledged on the parliamentary record.','A specific privatization benefit coincided with political support; causal advantage is disputed and the government says the award preceded the letter.','The donation–endorsement–EBRD loan–sale sequence is concrete but a reciprocal bargain is not established; later episodes in other jurisdictions are not averaged in.'],
  [[0,1],[0,1],[0,1]]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- BIRLA
FE='https://www.financialexpress.com/archive/kumar-mangalam-birla-embroiled-in-coalgate-case-with-cbi-filing-14th-fir/1183040/'
BT='https://m.businesstoday.in/story/cbi-closure-report-against-kumar-mangalam-birla-coal-scam/1/209815.html'
WIKI='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_coal_allocation_scam'
BB='https://www.barandbench.com/news/supreme-court-accepts-cbi-decision-to-clear-late-pm-manmohan-singh-of-coal-scam-allegations'
THE='https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/sc-closes-coal-block-allocation-case-against-former-pm-manmohan-singh/article71280626.ece'
IEP='https://www.indiaenvironmentportal.org.in/news-clippings/all-coal-allocations-since-1993-illegal-supreme-court'
CSE='https://www.cseindia.org/tag/mining-sector'
add('kumar-birla',
 'The Hindalco/Talabira II coal-block episode concerns India. In October 2013 the CBI registered a case alleging that Birla influenced the then coal secretary to get Hindalco added to a block meant for public-sector firms; the Aditya Birla Group denied it. In August 2014 the CBI filed a closure report. A special judge reportedly declined it and took cognizance in March 2015, and the Supreme Court stayed related summons. On 29 July 2026 the Supreme Court, in Manmohan Singh’s appeal, accepted the CBI closure reports and closed the matter on merits. Whether that order separately resolves Birla’s and Hindalco’s own position was not verified. The Supreme Court’s 2014 rulings held coal allocations since 1993 illegal and cancelled 214 blocks as a class, not as a finding against Birla. Birla’s government advisory roles are not scored.',
 'State coal-block allocation → corporate benefit; alleged influence on the secretary, closed by the CBI and not substantiated',
 'Allocation 2005; FIR October 2013; CBI closure report August 2014; special-court cognizance March 2015 (secondary); Supreme Court closure of the Singh appeal 29 July 2026',
 'Low / single episode, outcome for Birla personally not fully verified',
 [r('The CBI’s FIR (its 14th in the coal case) alleged criminal conspiracy and corruption: the Talabira II block in Odisha was allocated in 2005 to two public-sector firms, then Birla met the coal secretary, P.C. Parakh, after which the screening committee’s decision was overturned and Hindalco was added. The CBI said it had not quantified the loss. Searches were carried out at group offices.','The Financial Express: CBI FIR against Birla and Parakh',FE,'2013-10-16','Reported allegation by investigating agency','Archive page text read'),
  r('The CBI filed a closure report before Special Judge Bharat Parashar in August 2014, having booked Birla as representative of the Aditya Birla Group and Hindalco in October 2013.','PTI via Business Today',BT,'2014-08-29','Reported legal status (agency closure)','Page text read'),
  r('A Wikipedia compilation states that on 11 March 2015 a special CBI judge took cognizance against Hindalco, Birla, Parakh and others, and summoned Manmohan Singh; on 1 April 2015 the Supreme Court stayed the summons (the text names Singh and Parakh; one cited headline names Birla, a conflict not resolved here).','Wikipedia: Indian coal allocation scam (secondary compilation)',WIKI,'2015-03-11','Secondary compilation of legal status','Page text read in part (first 100,000 and part of remaining characters); original reports not opened'),
  r('The Supreme Court’s 2014 allocation ruling and cancellation: the Financial Express reported on 25 August 2014 that the Court declared all coal allocations since 1993 illegal, and the CSE release records that on 24 September 2014 it cancelled 214 coal blocks. These concern the allocation process as a class and name Hindalco only as a holder of another block (Mahan).','Financial Express via India Environment Portal; CSE release',IEP,'2014-08-25','Contextual court ruling (class-level, not Birla-specific)','Both pages read in part; CSE page at '+CSE+'; the judgment itself was not opened'),
  r('Bar and Bench reports that on 29 July 2026 a bench led by CJI Surya Kant allowed Manmohan Singh’s appeal against 2015 summons, saying there were no compelling reasons for the trial court to reject the CBI’s closure reports; it accepted the reports and closed the matter on merits. Singh’s counsel had earlier argued the allocation to Hindalco was an administrative decision.','Bar and Bench',BB,'2026-07-29','Reported Supreme Court order (appeal of a different person)','Article text read from the page’s structured data (paywalled page); order not opened')],
 [r('The Aditya Birla Group denied the charges, saying every process stipulated by the government’s coal-allocation policy was followed and that Hindalco was one of many companies being examined.','The Financial Express: group statement',FE,'2013-10-16','Subject response','Archive page text read'),
  r('The Hindu reports the Supreme Court agreed with two back-to-back CBI closure reports of 2014 giving Manmohan Singh a clean chit and found no sufficient material or good reason to register a corruption case against him; it also faulted the trial judge’s adverse observations. The reading does not by itself decide Birla’s own status.','The Hindu',THE,'2026-07-29','Legal status (different person)','Opening paragraphs read; remainder paywalled')],
 'Narrow episode review only. The primary 2014 and 2026 Supreme Court orders, the CBI closure report and the special court’s 2015 order were not opened; 2015 events rely on a secondary compilation. The 2026 order is reported in Singh’s appeal; its effect on Birla and Hindalco is unverified. The earlier Business Today (30 August 2014) receipt from wave 28 could not be re-fetched in this session and was replaced by the 29 August 2014 report. Vodafone Idea government relief measures and other Birla business with the state were not reviewed. Advisory roles are not points by themselves.',
 rubric=([1,2,1],[0,55],
  ['A specific meeting with the secretary is alleged by the CBI, but the CBI itself later closed the case and the Supreme Court accepted closure reports in the linked appeal.','The block was allocated to Hindalco alongside two public-sector firms, and allocations as a class were held illegal; any Birla-specific advantage is unquantified.','No exchange was substantiated; the agency’s closure and the 2026 acceptance weigh against it for the episode.'],
  [[0,1],[0,3],[0,1,4]]))

